Let $I(\lambda)=\int_{a(\lambda)}^{b(\lambda)}F(\lambda,u)\,\dd u$. Write $\partial F/\partial\lambda$ for the derivative of $F$ with respect to $\lambda$ with $u$ held fixed (a \emph{partial derivative}). Change $\lambda$ by a small $\epsilon$ and split the new range of integration into three pieces:
\begin{align}
I(\lambda+\epsilon)-I(\lambda)
&=\int_{a(\lambda)}^{b(\lambda)}\bigl[F(\lambda+\epsilon,u)-F(\lambda,u)\bigr]\dd u\notag\\
&\quad+\int_{b(\lambda)}^{b(\lambda+\epsilon)}F(\lambda+\epsilon,u)\,\dd u-\int_{a(\lambda)}^{a(\lambda+\epsilon)}F(\lambda+\epsilon,u)\,\dd u .
\end{align}
In the first integral, $F(\lambda+\epsilon,u)-F(\lambda,u)\approx\epsilon\,\partial F/\partial\lambda$. The second integral runs over a short interval of length $b(\lambda+\epsilon)-b(\lambda)\approx\epsilon\,b'(\lambda)$, on which $F\approx F(\lambda,b)$; similarly for the third. Dividing by $\epsilon$ and letting $\epsilon\to0$ gives \emph{Leibniz's rule}:
\begin{equation}
\frac{dI}{d\lambda}=F(\lambda,b)\,b'(\lambda)-F(\lambda,a)\,a'(\lambda)+\int_{a}^{b}\frac{\partial F}{\partial\lambda}\,\dd u .

